        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/997">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 997: Cross-Case Applicability and Comparability of an Information Environment Vulnerability Assessment Methodology: A Comparative Case Study</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/997</link>
	<description>This study examines the cross-case applicability and comparability of an information environment vulnerability assessment methodology in two functionally different university workplaces: a university library and a university institute. The study uses a comparative multi-case design to assess information providers and users through three complementary procedures: a context-specific semi-quantitative assessment, a common binary conformity assessment, and a weighted thematic analysis. Under the common descriptive classification framework, the overall outputs of all three procedures fall within the medium interval. The university institute has higher overall numerical values in each procedure, although the degree of direct comparability varies among them. At the perspective-specific level, the provider result for the university institute in Method 1 is 60.0% and falls within the high interval. Provider-side numerical outputs exceed user-side outputs in both workplaces. The comparison also shows that similar aggregate values can arise from different vulnerability structures, with the workplaces differing in the distribution of process, communication, documentation, technological, and information-interpretation vulnerabilities. The findings support procedural applicability and comparative interpretability across the two examined workplaces under a single-evaluator design. Broader transferability across independent organisations and assessors, however, remains to be established, and the assessment instrument requires further validation.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 997: Cross-Case Applicability and Comparability of an Information Environment Vulnerability Assessment Methodology: A Comparative Case Study</b></p>
