        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/996">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 996: Using Vision Images of First-Person for Gait Analysis</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/996</link>
	<description>Gait can demonstrate the motion characteristics of individuals and is used as a fundamental basis for assessing their physical conditions. In this paper, a new approach to gait analysis is presented by using a chest-mounted camera to collect images captured from a first-person perspective. After spatial gait measurements and temporal gait descriptors are extracted from those images, accurate gait parameters corresponding to personal medical information can be computed. Unlike traditional devices that need to stick sensors to the observed parts of the body, gait patterns can be obtained from a chest-mounted camera by the proposed new method. To prove the validity and robustness of this new approach for gait analysis, we considered various factors affecting the accuracy of gait parameters, including different individuals, genders, and ages. Additionally, the reference-system comparison and robustness experiments were conducted. Experimental results showed that this new approach can obtain accurate gait parameters close to those of expensive devices. Therefore, this approach can be further used for gait analysis to improve the quality of clinical medicine. This can also provide the advantage of convenience and suitability to apply for long-term healthcare at home.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 996: Using Vision Images of First-Person for Gait Analysis</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/996">doi: 10.3390/info17100996</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Tsorng-Lin Chia
		Chih-Yu Chang
		Ping-Sheng Huang
		</p>
	<p>Gait can demonstrate the motion characteristics of individuals and is used as a fundamental basis for assessing their physical conditions. In this paper, a new approach to gait analysis is presented by using a chest-mounted camera to collect images captured from a first-person perspective. After spatial gait measurements and temporal gait descriptors are extracted from those images, accurate gait parameters corresponding to personal medical information can be computed. Unlike traditional devices that need to stick sensors to the observed parts of the body, gait patterns can be obtained from a chest-mounted camera by the proposed new method. To prove the validity and robustness of this new approach for gait analysis, we considered various factors affecting the accuracy of gait parameters, including different individuals, genders, and ages. Additionally, the reference-system comparison and robustness experiments were conducted. Experimental results showed that this new approach can obtain accurate gait parameters close to those of expensive devices. Therefore, this approach can be further used for gait analysis to improve the quality of clinical medicine. This can also provide the advantage of convenience and suitability to apply for long-term healthcare at home.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

