	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 994: Structure-Aware Learning and Smart-Contract Enforcement for Cognitive IoT Admission Control</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/994</link>
	<description>Admission control governs quality of service (QoS) in cognitive IoT networks (CIoTNs) in which secondary nodes opportunistically share spectrum with preemptive primary users. The optimal policy is of threshold type, but the threshold table is indexed by the 2N fully occupied channel configurations, so both model-free learning and on-chain enforcement scale poorly in N. We show that this table has low intrinsic complexity: across 55 randomised continuous-time Markov decision process (CTMDP) instances with N&amp;amp;le;5, a monotone step function of the aggregate secondary-user drain rate &amp;amp;sigma;(n) with only 1.9&amp;amp;ndash;2.5 levels reproduces 78&amp;amp;ndash;88% of the entries exactly, every miss being a single queue slot. A quasi-static argument explains why &amp;amp;sigma;(n) is the right summary, and slowing the primary-user dynamics raises the exact-match rate above 99%. Certified exact solves up to N=12 show that the regularity persists but coarsens: the level count grows with the spread of channel rates (to 7.2 at N=12, but to only about three up to N=10 when the spread is held fixed), and two-slot misses appear from N=6. Used as an inductive bias in tabular Q-learning, index pooling did nothelp on our instances: it raised policy value loss relative to per-configuration read-out in every cell, because the visited queue prefixes were shorter than the thresholds and pooling propagated biased estimates, whereas a model-based certainty-equivalence controller fitted to the same observations recovered the optimal table in every run. Projecting learned value differences onto the concave cone is ineffective, and we report a negative result. A Solidity implementation measured on a local Ethereum Virtual Machine shows that index compression cuts policy-installation gas at N=16 by 241.8&amp;amp;times; for a two-level table and by an estimated 120&amp;amp;ndash;220&amp;amp;times; for the level counts our solves support. It keeps installation within one transaction where the full table exceeds the 224-gas per-transaction cap (N&amp;amp;ge;15), but adds 14.4&amp;amp;ndash;18.4k gas per decision: a feasibility mechanism, not a uniform improvement.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 994: Structure-Aware Learning and Smart-Contract Enforcement for Cognitive IoT Admission Control</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/994">doi: 10.3390/info17100994</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Yali Ren
		Ning Wang
		</p>
	<p>Admission control governs quality of service (QoS) in cognitive IoT networks (CIoTNs) in which secondary nodes opportunistically share spectrum with preemptive primary users. The optimal policy is of threshold type, but the threshold table is indexed by the 2N fully occupied channel configurations, so both model-free learning and on-chain enforcement scale poorly in N. We show that this table has low intrinsic complexity: across 55 randomised continuous-time Markov decision process (CTMDP) instances with N&amp;amp;le;5, a monotone step function of the aggregate secondary-user drain rate &amp;amp;sigma;(n) with only 1.9&amp;amp;ndash;2.5 levels reproduces 78&amp;amp;ndash;88% of the entries exactly, every miss being a single queue slot. A quasi-static argument explains why &amp;amp;sigma;(n) is the right summary, and slowing the primary-user dynamics raises the exact-match rate above 99%. Certified exact solves up to N=12 show that the regularity persists but coarsens: the level count grows with the spread of channel rates (to 7.2 at N=12, but to only about three up to N=10 when the spread is held fixed), and two-slot misses appear from N=6. Used as an inductive bias in tabular Q-learning, index pooling did nothelp on our instances: it raised policy value loss relative to per-configuration read-out in every cell, because the visited queue prefixes were shorter than the thresholds and pooling propagated biased estimates, whereas a model-based certainty-equivalence controller fitted to the same observations recovered the optimal table in every run. Projecting learned value differences onto the concave cone is ineffective, and we report a negative result. A Solidity implementation measured on a local Ethereum Virtual Machine shows that index compression cuts policy-installation gas at N=16 by 241.8&amp;amp;times; for a two-level table and by an estimated 120&amp;amp;ndash;220&amp;amp;times; for the level counts our solves support. It keeps installation within one transaction where the full table exceeds the 224-gas per-transaction cap (N&amp;amp;ge;15), but adds 14.4&amp;amp;ndash;18.4k gas per decision: a feasibility mechanism, not a uniform improvement.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Structure-Aware Learning and Smart-Contract Enforcement for Cognitive IoT Admission Control</dc:title>
