        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/991">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 991: Integrating Atmospheric Data into a Strategic Maritime Security Information Framework: An Exploratory Central Mediterranean Panel Study</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/991</link>
	<description>This study introduces a conceptual atmospheric information system framework for the strategic screening of maritime pressure, without entailing the implementation of a software platform or the validation of an operational tool. Integrating Copernicus-derived marine indicators, ERA5 atmospheric variables, and EUROSTAT socioeconomic data, we constructed a balanced country (Greece, Italy, Malta, and Spain)&amp;amp;ndash;year panel (2010&amp;amp;ndash;2024), analyzed through fixed-effects models and REPTree regression. We built a Maritime Structural Pressure Proxy (MSPP), which is treated as a formative indicator and subjected to sensitivity analysis regarding weights, normalization, and the definition of the economic component. To verify transferability, random 10-fold validation is supplemented by a leave-one-country-out (LOCO) approach. The integrated model yields an RMSE_LOCO of 0.098, compared to 0.130 for the meteorological model, and internal predictions are consistent across three of the four countries. The pruned regression tree, restricted to four leaves, achieves r = 0.890 and RMSE = 0.057 on the sample. Its Gross Fixed Capital Formation (GFCF) partitions are sample-specific and reflect the MSPP construction. The results support only the exploratory comparison of structural configurations and hypothesis formulation, rather than operational thresholds, SAR predictions, or causal inferences.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 991: Integrating Atmospheric Data into a Strategic Maritime Security Information Framework: An Exploratory Central Mediterranean Panel Study</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/991">doi: 10.3390/info17100991</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Florentina Loredana Dragomir-Constantin
		Alina Bărbulescu
		</p>
	<p>This study introduces a conceptual atmospheric information system framework for the strategic screening of maritime pressure, without entailing the implementation of a software platform or the validation of an operational tool. Integrating Copernicus-derived marine indicators, ERA5 atmospheric variables, and EUROSTAT socioeconomic data, we constructed a balanced country (Greece, Italy, Malta, and Spain)&amp;amp;ndash;year panel (2010&amp;amp;ndash;2024), analyzed through fixed-effects models and REPTree regression. We built a Maritime Structural Pressure Proxy (MSPP), which is treated as a formative indicator and subjected to sensitivity analysis regarding weights, normalization, and the definition of the economic component. To verify transferability, random 10-fold validation is supplemented by a leave-one-country-out (LOCO) approach. The integrated model yields an RMSE_LOCO of 0.098, compared to 0.130 for the meteorological model, and internal predictions are consistent across three of the four countries. The pruned regression tree, restricted to four leaves, achieves r = 0.890 and RMSE = 0.057 on the sample. Its Gross Fixed Capital Formation (GFCF) partitions are sample-specific and reflect the MSPP construction. The results support only the exploratory comparison of structural configurations and hypothesis formulation, rather than operational thresholds, SAR predictions, or causal inferences.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Integrating Atmospheric Data into a Strategic Maritime Security Information Framework: An Exploratory Central Mediterranean Panel Study</dc:title>
