        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/975">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 975: Benchmarking Named Entity Recognition for Low-Resource Tarifit Berber: A Comparative Evaluation of Traditional, Neural, Transformer-Based, and Span-Based Models</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/975</link>
	<description>Named Entity Recognition (NER) is a fundamental Natural Language Processing task, yet low-resource languages such as Tarifit Berber remain critically underexplored. This study introduces the first standardized and reproducible benchmark for Tarifit NER, addressing the lack of publicly available annotated corpora and unified evaluation protocols. A manually annotated corpus was developed using the IOB2 scheme, covering nine entity categories, and subjected to rigorous quality-assurance procedures, including data-leakage detection and out-of-vocabulary (OOV) analysis. Ten representative architectures were evaluated under an identical protocol, spanning traditional statistical models (CRF), neural sequence-labeling models (BiLSTM-CRF), multilingual Transformer models (mBERT, XLM-RoBERTa, RemBERT, LUKE), Arabic-oriented pretrained models (AraBERT, MARBERT, DarijaBERT), and the span-based GLiNER approach. Evaluation combined entity- and token-level metrics, category-wise analysis, lexical robustness across known, rare, and OOV entities, and bootstrap-based statistical reliability analysis. Results show that Transformer models consistently outperform traditional statistical and neural sequence-labeling models, with mBERT achieving the highest entity-level F1 score (95.76%), followed by MARBERT, DarijaBERT, and RemBERT. Location and Date were the most challenging categories, while GLiNER performed substantially worse under strict exact-match evaluation. These findings establish a reliable benchmark for Tarifit NER and provide a reusable foundation for future research in Amazigh and multilingual NLP.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-03</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 975: Benchmarking Named Entity Recognition for Low-Resource Tarifit Berber: A Comparative Evaluation of Traditional, Neural, Transformer-Based, and Span-Based Models</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/975">doi: 10.3390/info17100975</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Amar Amakssoum
		Mohamed Cherradi
		Anass El Haddadi
		Abdelkhalak Bahri
		</p>
	<p>Named Entity Recognition (NER) is a fundamental Natural Language Processing task, yet low-resource languages such as Tarifit Berber remain critically underexplored. This study introduces the first standardized and reproducible benchmark for Tarifit NER, addressing the lack of publicly available annotated corpora and unified evaluation protocols. A manually annotated corpus was developed using the IOB2 scheme, covering nine entity categories, and subjected to rigorous quality-assurance procedures, including data-leakage detection and out-of-vocabulary (OOV) analysis. Ten representative architectures were evaluated under an identical protocol, spanning traditional statistical models (CRF), neural sequence-labeling models (BiLSTM-CRF), multilingual Transformer models (mBERT, XLM-RoBERTa, RemBERT, LUKE), Arabic-oriented pretrained models (AraBERT, MARBERT, DarijaBERT), and the span-based GLiNER approach. Evaluation combined entity- and token-level metrics, category-wise analysis, lexical robustness across known, rare, and OOV entities, and bootstrap-based statistical reliability analysis. Results show that Transformer models consistently outperform traditional statistical and neural sequence-labeling models, with mBERT achieving the highest entity-level F1 score (95.76%), followed by MARBERT, DarijaBERT, and RemBERT. Location and Date were the most challenging categories, while GLiNER performed substantially worse under strict exact-match evaluation. These findings establish a reliable benchmark for Tarifit NER and provide a reusable foundation for future research in Amazigh and multilingual NLP.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Benchmarking Named Entity Recognition for Low-Resource Tarifit Berber: A Comparative Evaluation of Traditional, Neural, Transformer-Based, and Span-Based Models</dc:title>
